From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ps5cemu <ps5cemu@localhost>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 21:01:22 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] PS5: the aiming crosshair keeps its screen size, shows on the
 sky and is solid at 100%; built, untested on console

The marker at the arrow's target is BetterVR's own: DrawAimingArc draws a 0.16 m yellow
(255, 214, 64) wire sphere at the game's weapon target position. It is not the game's HUD
reticle, which is only ever a texture on the floating HUD panel. Being world-sized, it shrank
with distance; upstream skipped the synthetic target point, so an empty sky showed nothing; and
the xray pass multiplied its alpha by 0.35, so 100 % could never be solid.

Three settings beside Bow Aiming Arc: Aiming Crosshair Size (10-400 %, the radius), Aiming
Crosshair Color (White, Red, Green, Yellow, Cyan) and Aiming Crosshair Opacity (0-100 %, the
crosshair's final on-screen alpha, so 100 % is solid while it still draws through walls). The
base radius is a quarter of that 0.16 m, so Size 100 % is a quarter of the size BetterVR's own
marker had and 400 % reaches it again; a console check found the marker too big at the previous
size. The radius follows the distance from the aim's start to keep the on-screen size it has at
10 m, clamped to 0.1-20 times; Size scales it. The synthetic/fallback point draws too, at the
same size. The crosshair alone skips the 0.35 xray fade: DebugDraw::Sphere gains an xraySolid
flag that puts its lines in a fourth bucket, drawn through the xray pipeline with the scale at
1.0. The arc and its fall marker are untouched, and the setting keys are unchanged, so saved
settings load. The menu rows and the arc's step say crosshair, not dot.

At Yellow, Size 100 % and Opacity 10 % the crosshair is the old default brightness in the
default (70 %) arc mode: alpha 100 x 0.7 x 0.35 is 24.5 of 255, and 10 % is 25.5. The rows use the
existing percent slider and combo helpers, whose label and setting-name PushIDs give each row
its own focus, as 0046 did for the Vibration page.
